---
import { nav, site } from '../data/site';
---
<footer class="foot" data-tone="void">
  <div class="foot__grid">
    <p class="foot__about">
      {site.name} is an unofficial explainer of <cite>{site.film.name}</cite> ({site.film.year}), directed by {site.film.director}.
      It is not affiliated with the film or its studios. Line drawings and the Endurance ring are drawn in code for this site.
    </p>
    <nav class="foot__nav" aria-label="Footer">
      <ul>
        {nav.map((n) => (
          <li><a class="dilate draw" data-text={n.label} href={n.href}><span class="dilate__t">{n.label}</span></a></li>
        ))}
        <li><a class="dilate draw" data-text="Back to the top" href="#main"><span class="dilate__t">Back to the top</span></a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <p class="foot__colophon">Set in Archivo and IBM Plex Mono. Numbers marked ≈ are estimates; the film states the rest.</p>
    <p class="foot__colophon">Photographs: NASA/JPL-Caltech/Space Science Institute (Cassini), NASA Goddard (black hole simulations by Jeremy Schnittman and Brian P. Powell; Antarctica), NASA Ames Research Center (space colony study, 1976), NASA and ESA Hubble (eXtreme Deep Field, a spiral galaxy and the lensing cluster SDSS J1038+4849; the wormhole sphere and tunnel are rendered for this site from them), NASA ISS Expedition 40, Arthur Rothstein for the Farm Security Administration (Library of Congress), USDA. All public domain, toned for this site. Film stills of the Ranger, TARS, Gargantua and the watch, the production still of the tesseract set, and DNEG’s wormhole renders from Visualizing Interstellar’s Wormhole, are © Warner Bros. Entertainment and Paramount Pictures, shown for commentary.</p>
  </div>
</footer>
